    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Sharecropping and the crop lien",
    text: "After emancipation, freedpeople wanted land of their own but rarely got it. Instead, sharecropping spread across the South: a family farmed a plot of a landlord's land and received a share of the crop, usually half, with the landlord providing house, mule and seed. Supplies through the year came on credit from a merchant or the landlord's store, secured by a lien on the crop at high interest; at settlement time after the harvest, many families found they still owed. Many poor white families became sharecroppers and tenants too. The boll weevil, the Depression and mechanisation pushed millions to migrate north after 1915.",
