    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Getting through the Depression",
    text: "After 1929, unemployment in the United States reached a quarter of the workforce by 1933, and in Canada nearly as much. Banks failed and took savings with them; farms were foreclosed. Families doubled up with relatives, took in boarders, planted gardens, canned vegetables, made clothes from flour sacks and bought on credit at the corner store. The homeless built shanty towns, 'Hoovervilles'. Drought on the southern Plains drove the Dust Bowl migration to California. From 1933 the New Deal brought relief payments and work programmes: the Works Progress Administration employed some 8.5 million people over its life building roads, schools and parks.",
