    evidence: "documented",
    title: "The country store and the mail-order catalogue",
    text: "In rural America the general store sold everything from coffee, sugar and cloth to nails and patent medicine, and often bought farmers' eggs, butter and furs in exchange. Most customers bought on account and settled after harvest. The store usually housed the post office, and men gathered around the stove to talk politics. Yankee pedlars carried tinware and notions into remote districts before 1850. Rural Free Delivery from 1896 brought mail to farm doors, and mail-order catalogues from Montgomery Ward (1872) and Sears, Roebuck (1888) let farm families buy the same goods as city people, at prices storekeepers could not match.",
    wiki: ["General store", "Rural Free Delivery", "Sears catalog"],
