    title: "The one-room school and McGuffey's Readers",
    text: "From the 1830s, reformers like Horace Mann pushed for free, tax-supported common schools. In the countryside, children of all ages shared a one-room schoolhouse with a single teacher, often a young woman, who kept order, taught reading, writing, arithmetic and geography, and tended the stove. Pupils recited lessons from McGuffey's Eclectic Readers, which sold over 100 million copies. The school term was fitted around farm work. By 1918 every state had compulsory attendance laws. In the South, Black children attended segregated schools with far less funding, many built with the help of the Rosenwald Fund from 1917.",
