    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Old age before and after Social Security",
    text: "Most old Americans in the 19th century lived with a married child, often the one who would inherit the farm in exchange for their care, a bargain sometimes written into deeds. Those without family or means could end up in the county poor farm, where the elderly poor worked as they could. Union veterans' pensions after the Civil War became a large old-age support system for northern families. In cities, industrial work pushed older workers out. The Social Security Act of 1935 created old-age insurance and assistance, with the first monthly checks paid in 1940, though farm and domestic workers were excluded.",
