    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Calling, courtship and coverture",
    text: "In 19th-century America, young people chose their own partners, within the limits of family approval and class. A young man would 'call' on a young woman at her family's home, under watch; church socials, dances and sleigh rides were other places to meet. Under the law of coverture, a married woman's property and earnings belonged to her husband; married women's property acts, starting in Mississippi in 1839 and New York in 1848, slowly changed this. In the 1920s, dating, going out to movies and dance halls, replaced calling. Divorce, though rare, rose steadily from the 1880s.",
