    title: "Whaling from Nantucket and New Bedford",
    text: "Nantucket whalers began hunting sperm whales in deep water around 1712, and by the 1840s New Bedford was the world's whaling capital, with over 700 American ships. Voyages to the Pacific lasted three or four years. Crews of about thirty, including many Wampanoag, Cape Verdean, Black and Pacific Islander men, chased whales in small boats, struck them with hand harpoons and lances, then cut up the carcass alongside the ship and boiled the blubber down in the tryworks on deck. Crew were paid a 'lay', a share of the profit, which after deductions could leave little. Petroleum after 1859 undermined the industry.",
