    evidence: "documented",
    title: "The village blacksmith",
    text: "Every American village and plantation needed a blacksmith, who shod horses and oxen, made and mended tools, hinges, nails, chains, wagon tyres and plough parts. Iron came as bar stock from bloomeries and later from rolling mills. A smith worked with a charcoal or coal forge, bellows, anvil and hammers, often with an apprentice or son. On southern plantations, many blacksmiths were enslaved men whose skill was hired out. From the late 19th century factory-made tools, nails and machinery reduced the smith to repair and horseshoeing, and the automobile then turned many forges into garages.",
